cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Severity Without Frequency: A Multi-Country Validation of a Cycling Routing Risk Score"
type: article
authors:
  - given-names: Victor
    family-names: Rotariu
    email: victorrotariu@gmail.com
abstract: >-
  A five-country, 13-region counter-exposure panel of 2,034 sites is used to
  test whether a deployed, safety-weighted cycling routing risk score
  predicts crash frequency and crash severity (killed-or-seriously-injured
  outcomes) differently, once cyclist exposure is accounted for. The score's
  relationship to raw crash frequency is weak and inconsistent; its
  relationship to crash severity is significant at every cut-point tested,
  strengthens under a later, independently re-scored model generation, and is
  the first to survive full road-class control, concentrated at mid-block
  locations rather than junctions.
keywords:
  - cycling safety
  - exposure-adjusted crash risk
  - road-class control
  - severity versus frequency
  - routing risk score
